# The character and the suit both arms of the quality-immunity check share.
# Included by quality-self-immune-plain.keys and quality-self-immune-quality.keys.
# Read by tools/check_quality_self_immune.sh (bd inc-to9x, inc-aoi4i).
#
# THE CHARACTER is the frozen fixture orc-mage-seed5-opt0822, loaded by the
# check script (INCURSION_LOAD), not generated in the run: a generated
# character's Charisma and Decipher Script vary with the seed.
#
# WHY THE BURN IS A WIZARD COMMAND. A player-driven blast reaches worn gear only
# through the equipment block in Magic::Blast (src/Effects.cpp:395-421), whose
# save and coin tosses have no control. The wizard command "Burn Worn Armour"
# (src/Debug.cpp, inc-aoi4i) sends a fixed 10 points of fire straight to the
# worn suit, with no actor and no random number. It replaces the three hell
# hounds this script used to summon, whose breath no control reaches.
#
# WHY A PLAIN LEATHER SUIT. MaterialHardness gives MAT_LEATHER a fire hardness
# of 0 (src/Item.cpp:1258), so every point of fire reaches it, and Item::Damage
# names the material in its own numbers line -- "vs. 0 (leather)" against
# "vs. Immune (leather)" is the whole measurement. A plain suit from the
# acquisition menu is made by Item::Create alone (src/Term.cpp:3287): no
# MakeMagical, so no random curse, and no plus.
#
# WHY LEVEL FIVE. Enchant Armour needs the item's level <= the reader's level
# plus Charisma modifier, and the mage starts at level one. 'd' opens the
# character screen and 'G' gains a level; Free Advancement (wizard switch 3)
# makes it free.

@until "Things in View" ESC

# Wizard switches, counted from the top of the list in src/Tables.cpp:2679:
#   3 Free Advancement.
w
y
@choose "Wizard Mode Switches"
SPACE
DOWN*2
SPACE
ESC
@until "Things in View" ESC

# Four free levels. 'd' opens the character screen and 'G'
# gains a level; SPACE leaves it.
d
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
G
@while "Bonus To Which Attribute" a
@while "Choose a feat" a
@while "Choose a bonus metamagic Feat" a
@while "You feel" ESC
SPACE
@until "Things in View" ESC

# --- the suit whose hit points are measured -----------------------------
# Column 15 of the acquisition prompt is Armours. The list shows the selected
# row and the row under it, so @until stops with "Leather armour" one row below
# the cursor and the extra DOWN selects it. No "Enter Item Level" prompt
# follows, because a plain leather suit has no inherent plus.
w
@choose "Item Acquisition"
RIGHT*15
@until "Leather armour" DOWN
DOWN
ENTER
j
SPACE
@while "two-handed" y
@until "Things in View" ESC

# An unidentified item's page hides its hit points, and the hit points are half
# the measurement.
w
@choose "Identify Whole Pack"
@until "Things in View" ESC
